{0 JavaScript globals}

Workero has typed bindings for four JavaScript globals. You do not need
[Js_of_ocaml] to use them.

{1 Timer}

{!Workero.Timer} wraps [setTimeout] and [setInterval]. Delays are in
milliseconds.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  Promise.await (Timer.sleep 100);
  let ticks = ref 0 in
  let interval = Timer.set_interval (fun () -> incr ticks) 20 in
  Promise.await (Timer.sleep 110);
  Timer.clear_interval interval;
  Response.text (Printf.sprintf "ticks: %d\n" !ticks)

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Call timers only from a handler or from {!Workero.Context.wait_until}. The
Workers runtime refuses timers at startup. A callback runs outside the
handler, so it cannot call {!Workero.Promise.await}.

{1 Date}

{!Workero.Date.now} is [Date.now()]. {!Workero.Date.to_iso_string} is
[new Date(t).toISOString()]. Times are milliseconds since the Unix epoch, as a
[float]. In Workers the clock moves only after I/O. It does not measure CPU
time.

{1 Crypto}

{!Workero.Crypto.random_uuid} returns a random version 4 UUID.
{!Workero.Crypto.random_bytes} returns cryptographically random bytes. It
accepts at most 65536 bytes per call.

{2 Subtle crypto}

{!Workero.Crypto} wraps a first subset of [crypto.subtle]. Data goes in and out
as OCaml strings that hold raw bytes. The functions suspend the request while
the runtime works, so call them inside a handler.

{[
let sign_and_check secret body =
  let key = Crypto.Key.import_hmac Crypto.Sha256 secret in
  let signature = Crypto.hmac_sign key body in
  Crypto.hmac_verify key ~signature body
]}

- {!Workero.Crypto.digest}: SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384 and SHA-512, chosen by
  {!Workero.Crypto.hash}.
- {!Workero.Crypto.Key.import_hmac}, {!Workero.Crypto.hmac_sign} and
  {!Workero.Crypto.hmac_verify}.
- {!Workero.Crypto.Key.import_aes_gcm}, {!Workero.Crypto.aes_gcm_encrypt} and
  {!Workero.Crypto.aes_gcm_decrypt}. The cipher text ends with the 16-byte tag.
  Decryption gives [None] when authentication fails.
- {!Workero.Crypto.pbkdf2} and {!Workero.Crypto.hkdf} derive bytes.

A [Key.t] is a non-extractable [CryptoKey]. Its type names the algorithm, so
the compiler rejects an HMAC key in an AES-GCM function. Bad arguments that the
library can check raise [Invalid_argument]. A rejection from the runtime raises
{!Workero.Js_error}. Deployed Workers allow at most 100000 PBKDF2 iterations.

{1 Console}

{!Workero.Console} writes log entries. See {{!page-logging}Logging}.

For other globals, use raw interop. See {{!page-interop}JavaScript interop}.
